<h2>The two answers the Bench gave</h2>
<p>The first answer was preclusive. The release argument built on the irregular remand and the set-aside cognizance had already been considered and rejected by the single judge, and the challenge to that ruling had failed in the Supreme Court. The Bench recorded that the petitioner was trying to re-open an issue which already stands determined, and that it was not sitting in appeal over a co-ordinate ruling — a ground sufficient by itself to dismiss the writ.</p>
<p>The second answer went to the merits. Relying on <em>Judgebir Singh</em>, (2023) 17 SCC 48, which follows <em>Suresh Kumar Bhikamchand Jain</em> (2013), the Bench reiterated that the filing of the chargesheet or complaint within the 60/90-day window is what defeats default bail. Cognizance — a judicial act that may be delayed, deferred for a pre-cognizance hearing, or even set aside and re-taken — is not the event the statute watches. Since the ED complaint had been filed within the Section 187 period, the continued remand was lawful, and the petitioner's remedy lay in a bail application on merits.</p>

<h2>Why the distinction matters</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Event</th><th>Effect on default bail</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Complaint or chargesheet filed within 60/90 days</td><td>Right to default bail never crystallises</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cognizance taken late, or deferred for a Section 223 BNSS hearing</td><td>No effect — remand continues under the ordinary law</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cognizance order set aside on procedural grounds</td><td>Cognizance is considered afresh; no fresh default-bail right arises</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Complaint or chargesheet not filed in time</td><td>Indefeasible right to release arises, if claimed before filing</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The judgment also illustrates a quieter point of PMLA practice under the new codes: the pre-cognizance hearing under Section 223 BNSS is a genuine procedural safeguard — it undid a cognizance order here — but it is not a lever for release. Procedural irregularity in remand, once cured or superseded, does not convert into liberty.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> For accused persons in long ED investigations, the realistic sequence is to watch the 60/90-day window closely and apply the moment it lapses; once the complaint is on file, energy is better spent on a merits bail application under Section 45 PMLA than on collateral writs attacking cognizance. The findings here are confined to the legality of remand and decide nothing about the merits of the money-laundering allegation.</p>
</div>
